WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.949
Good morning, Hank. It's Tuesday. I really liked your video on Friday,

00:00:01.949 --> 00:00:04.080
I thought you did a great job explaining Facebook's IPO,

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:05.999
and why stock markets are so important.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.840
But then, at the end of the video, you said this,

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:10.140
"A company that gives me a free service,

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:11.100
that I use,

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:11.729
and have never

00:00:11.729 --> 00:00:13.420
and will never pay for,

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:14.980
is somehow worth

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:16.120
one hundred

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:17.780
billion dollars.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:18.760
What?"

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.080
Now Hank, I don't know if you intended that as a rhetorical question,

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.560
but today I am going to answer your

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:24.200
"what?"

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:25.650
And here's the thing,

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:27.280
broadly speaking there are two kinds of companies in this world:

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:29.060
there are companies that sell you things

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:31.540
and then there are companies that sell YOU.

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:32.340
Actually come to think of it,

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:33.720
there's a third kind of company,

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:36.120
the kind that sells both things and you.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.580
Like cable companies, for instance.

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:38.580
Cable companies are like,

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.140
"Hey, if you give us 80 bucks a month

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:44.300
We'll give you the privilege of watching content funded primarily by advertising."

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:44.910
But anyway, Hank,

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:46.649
I believe that the Internet Maxim is true:

00:00:46.649 --> 00:00:48.520
if you're not paying for a service,

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:49.260
YOU

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:50.640
are the product for sale.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.560
Facebook is worth a hundred billion dollars because,

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.509
well actually it's not worth a hundred billion dollars,

00:00:54.509 --> 00:00:56.680
but it's worth whatever it's worth because of you!

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:58.680
I mean Facebook could MySpace tomorrow,

00:00:58.680 --> 00:00:59.940
and it would be worth nothing.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:00.960
That means that companies like

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.660
Facebook or Google or Twitter or Tumblr

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:04.409
have to listen to their users which is a good thing.

00:01:04.409 --> 00:01:05.520
But make no mistake,

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:06.960
the reason Facebook is valuable is

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.240
because you use Facebook

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:09.740
and you are valuable.

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:11.800
I mean you in particular, Hank, but also other people.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:12.960
The stuff that you upload to Facebook,

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:14.860
whether it's wall posts or photographs or videos,

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:18.269
is valuable because it brings other people in to the site to look at that stuff,

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:20.400
and then they in turn upload their stuff,

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.380
which brings yet more people into the site.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:24.960
And everything that you upload helps Facebook know more about you,

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:29.240
which, in turn, allows Facebook to know what you are tempted to buy and encourage you to buy it.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:32.120
Now Hank, I know that you like everyone on the planet will insist that

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:33.860
you don't even look at ads.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:37.260
And you'll insist that you aren't even conscious of what's getting hawked over there

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:38.240
or possibly over there,

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:39.620
(damn you, spatial intelligence!)

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:42.980
and you'll inevitably believe that you get the better end of the bargain because, of course,

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:44.600
you get the thing you want for free

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.470
and the ad has no effect on you.

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:47.400
Well Hank, let me just point out that

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.580
a.) that is exactly what advertisers want you to think

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:55.120
and b.) corporations actually have a really good idea of how advertisements affect your behavior.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.820
In fact, there are many thousands of people

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:00.920
who are working full time to make sure that the ads you see are worth more than they cost.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:01.940
To put it succinctly,

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:03.100
almost by definition,

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.620
advertisers buy you for less than you're worth.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:09.840
I mean Hank, corporations spent $498 billion on advertising last year.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.660
And you may think that that advertising doesn't affect your behavior,

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:15.820
but there is HALF A TRILLION DOLLARS that says otherwise.

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:17.440
You and I and anyone who uses

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:20.040
Facebook or Twitter or Google or Angry Birds

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:21.760
has agreed to be commodified.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.360
I'm not saying that's a bad decision,

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:24.680
but it is a decision

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:27.620
and it's one that should be made thoughtfully and consciously.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:29.200
Hank, we've never been totally comfortable with ads,

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.380
but it's always felt like the best solution to allow us

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:33.520
to make the stuff that we like with people we like.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:34.689
But I don't know. Nerdfighters,

00:02:34.689 --> 00:02:36.200
I'm interested in hearing your thoughts on this.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:39.960
Should Chrysler and super-pacs and scammy online dating sites

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:41.700
fund Nerdfighteria?

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:43.640
And should we be okay with being the product?

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:45.640
I look forward to continuing that conversation in comments.

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:47.580
Hank, thanks for teaching me what an IPO is,

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:50.000
DFTBA, I will see you on Friday.

